insert into public.devotionals (n, title, body, today, pray) values
(52, $t$In season, out of season$t$, $t$Paul wrote these words to Timothy near the end of his life. Be ready "in season" and "out of season," when it's convenient and when it's not.

Opportunities to speak about Jesus rarely come when we feel ready. They come in the middle of a busy afternoon, at the end of a long day, or in a hospital waiting room.

Notice too: "with all longsuffering." Readiness doesn't mean pushiness. It means being willing to speak patiently, whenever the door opens.

Be ready today. The moment may come when you least expect it.$t$, $t$Keep one person in mind all day, and be ready if they bring up something that opens a door.$t$, $t$Lord, keep me ready, in season and out.$t$),
(53, $t$As to the Lord$t$, $t$Paul wrote this to servants doing ordinary, unseen work. Do it with your whole heart, he says, as if you're doing it for the Lord, because you are.

This turns ordinary work into worship and witness. The way you do your job, care for your family, and treat the people around you all matters to God.

It also matters to the people watching. Coworkers notice who works hard without complaining. Neighbors notice who shows up to help. A life lived "as to the Lord" makes people curious about the Lord.

Whatever you do today, do it for Him.$t$, $t$Pick one routine task today and do it with care, as an offering to God.$t$, $t$Lord, let my ordinary work today be done for You.$t$),
(54, $t$He saw them$t$, $t$Before Jesus preached, healed, or sent anyone out, He saw. He didn't see a crowd to manage or a schedule to keep. He saw tired, scattered people who had no one looking after them, and it moved Him.

Most of us walk past people all day without really seeing them. The cashier, the coworker, the neighbor who waves from the driveway. They're background to our day. But every one of them has a story, a burden, and a soul Jesus cares about.

Compassion starts with attention. You can't love someone you haven't noticed. Today, ask God to slow you down long enough to see one person the way He sees them.$t$, $t$Think of one person you see often but don't really know. Add them to Tend, even if all you know is their first name.$t$, $t$Lord, give me Your eyes for the people I pass today.$t$),
(55, $t$Come down, Zacchaeus$t$, $t$A whole crowd lined that road in Jericho. Jesus stopped for one man in a tree. He called him by name and invited Himself to dinner.

Zacchaeus was the person everyone else had written off. Nobody expected Jesus to choose his house. But the most unlikely person in town was the one ready to meet Him.

We tend to pick the people we think are "open." Jesus kept surprising His disciples by stopping for the ones they had overlooked. Sometimes the person who seems furthest from God is the one already climbing a tree to get a look.

Is there someone you've quietly decided wouldn't be interested? Hold that assumption loosely. Let God decide who's ready.$t$, $t$Look through your people for someone you've stopped reaching out to. Set one small next step for them.$t$, $t$Jesus, help me stop for the people I would have passed by.$t$),
(56, $t$Looking and loving$t$, $t$The rich young ruler asked Jesus a sincere question, and Jesus gave him a hard answer. The man walked away sad. But before Jesus said a word of challenge, Mark tells us something beautiful: Jesus looked at him and loved him.

Jesus didn't love him because he said yes. He loved him knowing he would walk away.

That's freeing for us. Our love for people can't depend on how they respond. Some of the people you're tending may never come to faith on your timeline. Some may push back. Love them anyway, and keep loving them.

The truth Jesus spoke came after the look of love, not instead of it. People can usually tell which one comes first.$t$, $t$Pick someone who has pushed back on faith. Do one kind thing for them that asks for nothing in return.$t$, $t$Jesus, help me love people before they respond and after.$t$),
(57, $t$When he saw him$t$, $t$The priest saw the wounded man. So did the Levite. Both crossed the road. The Samaritan saw the same man, and he stopped.

Seeing isn't enough by itself. The question is what we do next. The Samaritan was on a journey with his own plans. He let them be interrupted.

Most interruptions don't look like a man on the side of the road. They look like a text from a friend having a hard week, or a coworker who lingers after a meeting. These are small moments where we can cross toward someone or walk on by.

You don't have to fix everything. The Samaritan bandaged wounds, paid for a room, and promised to come back. Faithful love is often just showing up again.$t$, $t$Is anyone in your people going through something hard? Log what you know, and set a reminder to check back.$t$, $t$Lord, make me willing to be interrupted.$t$),
(58, $t$Not far from any of us$t$, $t$Paul told the people of Athens that God had set the times and places where people live, so that they would seek Him. Your neighborhood, your workplace, your kids' team: none of that is random.

The people around you are there on purpose. God has placed you near them, and He is already near them too. He isn't far from your coworker who never mentions faith, or your neighbor who seems uninterested. He's closer than their next breath.

That means you aren't bringing God to them. You're joining what He's already doing in their lives. Your part is to be faithful, pray, and speak when the door opens.$t$, $t$Think about where you spend most of your week. Add one person from there you'd like to know better.$t$, $t$Father, thank You for placing me near people You love.$t$),
(59, $t$First of all$t$, $t$Paul could have started with strategy, programs, or better arguments. Instead he says, "first of all," pray.

Prayer isn't the warm-up before the real work of evangelism. It is the real work. When we pray for someone, we're asking the only One who can change a heart to do what we never could.

Prayer also changes us. It's hard to stay annoyed with a coworker you're praying for by name every morning. It's hard to give up on a family member you keep bringing to God. Praying for people softens our hearts toward them, and that often shows up in how we treat them.

If you feel stuck with someone and don't know what to say, start here. Pray first. Talk second.$t$, $t$Open your prayer list and pray for each name slowly, one at a time.$t$, $t$Lord, make prayer my first move, not my last resort.$t$),
(60, $t$My heart's desire$t$, $t$Paul's people had opposed him, beaten him, and driven him out of town after town. Still, his heart's desire was for them to be saved.

Notice the order: heart's desire, then prayer. Paul didn't pray out of duty. He prayed because he wanted it. His prayers came from real longing.

It's possible to keep a prayer list and feel very little. That's honest, and God can work with honesty. But we can also ask Him to give us the desire Paul had. We can ask God to make us want what He wants for the people we love.

When your heart is cold toward someone, don't stop praying. Pray for them, and pray for your own heart too.$t$, $t$Choose one person on your list and tell God plainly what you hope happens in their life.$t$, $t$Father, give me Your heart for the people I'm praying for.$t$),
(61, $t$Open a door$t$, $t$Paul was writing from prison. He didn't ask the Colossians to pray for his release. He asked them to pray for an open door to talk about Jesus.

We often wait for the perfect opening and feel guilty when it doesn't come. But Paul shows us something better: ask God to open the door. Then watch for it.

Doors usually open in ordinary ways. A friend mentions a hard diagnosis. A coworker asks what you did this weekend. A neighbor brings up something they saw in the news. These are invitations if we're paying attention.

You don't need to force a conversation. Pray for the door, then walk through it gently when God opens it.$t$, $t$Pray for an open door with one specific person this week. Write their name in your prayer list if it isn't there yet.$t$, $t$Lord, open a door this week, and give me courage to walk through it.$t$),
(62, $t$Don't lose heart$t$, $t$Jesus told the story of a widow who kept coming back to an unjust judge until he finally gave in. Luke tells us exactly why Jesus told it: so we would keep praying and not give up.

Praying for people far from God can be discouraging. We pray for years and see little change. We start to wonder if it matters.

It does. Many believers can name someone who prayed for them for a long time before they came to faith. A mother, a grandparent, a friend who never stopped. Those prayers were not wasted. They were seeds in the ground.

God isn't an unjust judge. He's a good Father who hears every prayer. Keep praying.$t$, $t$Find the name you've prayed for the longest. Pray for them again today, with hope.$t$, $t$Father, keep me praying when I don't see anything changing.$t$),
(63, $t$Light in the dark$t$, $t$Sometimes we think if we found the perfect argument, people would believe. But Paul says the real problem is spiritual blindness. A better explanation can't cure blindness by itself.

That's why prayer matters so much. Two verses later, Paul says the God who said "Let light shine out of darkness" is the same God who shines in our hearts. He did it for you. He can do it for them.

This takes the pressure off. You are not responsible for opening anyone's eyes. You're responsible for loving them, praying for them, and telling them the truth with kindness. God gives the light.$t$, $t$Pray that God would open the eyes of one person you're tending to see Jesus clearly.$t$, $t$Lord, shine Your light into the hearts of the people I love.$t$),
(64, $t$Every remembrance$t$, $t$Paul's prayers for the Philippians were full of thanks. Every time he thought of them, he was grateful.

Prayer for others can slowly turn into a list of requests and worries. Thanksgiving brings it back to life. Thank God for the people you're tending: their humor, their kindness, the questions they ask. Thank Him for the ways He's already answered.

It also helps to remember answered prayers. When a prayer request gets answered, mark it in Tend. Over time you'll have a record of God's faithfulness. On discouraging days, that record will remind you that He hears.

Gratitude keeps prayer from becoming a burden. It reminds us that these people are gifts, not projects.$t$, $t$Look back at your prayer requests. Mark any that have been answered and thank God for each one.$t$, $t$Thank You, Lord, for every person You've put in my life.$t$),
(65, $t$A reason for the hope$t$, $t$Peter assumes people will ask. When believers live with real hope, especially in hard times, others notice. Sooner or later someone says, "How are you so calm about this?"

Being ready doesn't mean having a speech memorized. It means knowing why you hope in Jesus well enough to say it simply. What did He save you from? What difference does He make on an ordinary Tuesday?

Peter also tells us how to answer: with gentleness and respect. Winning the argument isn't the goal. Loving the person is. A kind answer that's a little clumsy does more good than a polished one delivered with a sharp edge.$t$, $t$In two or three sentences, write down why you hope in Jesus. Read it out loud once so it feels natural.$t$, $t$Lord, make me ready, and make me gentle.$t$),
(66, $t$One thing I know$t$, $t$The man born blind couldn't answer the religious leaders' theological questions. He didn't need to. He knew what had happened to him.

You don't have to be a Bible scholar to tell someone about Jesus. You have something no one can argue with: your own story. What was your life like? How did you meet Jesus? What's different now?

Your story doesn't have to be dramatic. "I was anxious and empty, and Jesus gave me peace" is a real testimony. So is "I grew up in church, but faith became my own when..."

People can debate ideas all day. It's harder to argue with "I was blind, and now I see."$t$, $t$Think of one person who has never heard how you came to faith. Ask God for a chance to tell them.$t$, $t$Jesus, help me tell what You've done for me simply and honestly.$t$),
(67, $t$Swift to hear$t$, $t$We often think evangelism is mostly talking. But some of the most powerful moments come from listening.

When you really listen, people feel valued. They tell you what they're afraid of, what hurts, and what questions they carry. Those are the places where the gospel speaks most clearly. If you talk too soon, you may answer questions they never asked.

Listening also builds trust. Most people have never had a Christian ask about their life and actually wait for the answer. Be that person.

Ask good questions. "What's been the hardest part of this year?" "What do you think happens after we die?" "Have you ever wondered about God?" Then be quiet and listen.$t$, $t$After your next real conversation, log what the person shared, what they care about, and any questions they asked.$t$, $t$Lord, help me listen more than I talk.$t$),
(68, $t$Come and see$t$, $t$Nathanael was skeptical. Philip didn't argue with him. He just said, "Come and see."

Sometimes the best thing we can do is invite. Invite someone to church, to a small group, to dinner with your family, or to read a Gospel with you. Let them see Jesus at work in real people.

Invitations can feel risky. They might say no. That's okay. A no today isn't a no forever, and most people are glad to be asked even if they decline. Many people who now follow Jesus came because someone simply invited them.

You don't have to answer every objection first. Come and see is enough.$t$, $t$Who could you invite to something this week? Add an Invite next step for them.$t$, $t$Lord, give me courage to invite, and leave the response to You.$t$),
(69, $t$Go home and tell$t$, $t$The man Jesus freed from demons begged to go with Him. Jesus said no. He sent him home instead.

That's surprising. Wouldn't he be more useful traveling with Jesus? But Jesus knew where his witness mattered most: with the people who had known him before.

Your family and old friends saw who you were before Jesus changed you. That makes them the hardest people to talk to and the ones most likely to notice the difference. You don't need a stage. You need to go home and tell them what the Lord has done.

Be patient with family. They may need to see change over time before they believe it. Keep loving them.$t$, $t$Is there a family member you've never really shared your faith with? Add them to Tend and start praying.$t$, $t$Lord, help me be faithful with the people closest to me.$t$),
(70, $t$Sow in the morning$t$, $t$A farmer doesn't know which seeds will grow, so he keeps sowing. Morning and evening, he scatters seed and trusts that some of it will take root.

We don't know which conversation will matter. The invitation you almost didn't send. The text you thought was too small. The prayer you prayed half asleep. You may never know which one God used.

So keep sowing. Don't wait for perfect conditions or a sure response. Small, steady faithfulness over months and years adds up to a lot of seed in the ground.

That's what Tend is for: helping you keep sowing, a little at a time, with the people God has given you.$t$, $t$Look at your next steps for this week. Do one that you've been putting off.$t$, $t$Lord, help me keep sowing even when I can't see what's growing.$t$),
(71, $t$While he was a great way off$t$, $t$The father in Jesus' story had been watching the road. When he saw his son far off, he ran to him.

This is how God feels about the people you're praying for. He's watching the road for them. He longs for them to come home even more than you do.

Some of the people you love are a great way off right now. They may seem uninterested, or even hostile. But distance doesn't scare God. One turn toward home, and He runs.

When you feel hopeless about someone, picture the father running down the road. That's the God you're praying to.$t$, $t$Pray for the person in your life who seems furthest from God. Ask the Father to bring them home.$t$, $t$Father, thank You for running toward people who are far away.$t$),
(72, $t$Given to hospitality$t$, $t$The phrase "given to hospitality" is active. Paul isn't just saying "be open to guests if they show up." He means pursue it, go after it.

Some of the most important conversations about faith happen around a table. Food slows people down. A meal says, "I want to spend time with you," without any pressure.

Hospitality doesn't need a clean house or a fancy dinner. Pizza on paper plates works. So does coffee on the porch or burgers in the backyard. People remember being welcomed far more than they remember the menu.

Opening your home can feel vulnerable. That's part of why it means so much. You're letting people into your real life.$t$, $t$Who could you have over in the next couple of weeks? Set up a Meetup and pick a day.$t$, $t$Lord, make my home a place where people feel welcome.$t$),
(73, $t$Strangers at the table$t$, $t$It's easy to show hospitality to people we already know and like. The writer of Hebrews pushes us further: welcome strangers.

Think about who is new around you. The new family on your street. The new hire at work. The person who sat alone at church last Sunday. They're often hoping someone will reach out, and most people won't.

Welcoming a stranger can be as simple as learning their name and remembering it next time. That small act tells someone they matter.

You never know who you're welcoming. Abraham served three visitors and found himself hosting the Lord. Every person you welcome bears God's image.$t$, $t$Think of someone new in your life. Add them to Tend with how you met and one thing you learned about them.$t$, $t$Lord, help me notice the new people around me.$t$),
(74, $t$By this all will know$t$, $t$Jesus said the world would recognize His followers by one thing: their love for each other.

That means how we treat our church family is part of our witness. When a friend who doesn't know Jesus sees believers caring for each other, forgiving each other, and showing up for each other, they see something of Jesus.

The opposite is true too. Gossip, cliques, and coldness in the church push people away.

This is why getting to know your church family matters. It isn't separate from reaching people. It's part of it. A church where people truly know and love each other is a place where outsiders feel at home.$t$, $t$Open your Church family tab. Pick one person you don't know well and reach out this week.$t$, $t$Jesus, let our love for each other point people to You.$t$),
(75, $t$Brotherly love$t$, $t$Paul uses family language for the church. We aren't just people who attend the same service. We're brothers and sisters.

But it's possible to sit near someone for years and never really know them. You know their face and maybe their name, but not their story, their struggles, or how to pray for them.

Families take time. Ask someone after church how their week really went. Learn their kids' names. Remember the surgery they mentioned and ask how it went. These small things build real affection.

"Preferring one another" means putting others first. Go out of your way for someone in your church this week, even in a small way.$t$, $t$Add a church family member's prayer request to Tend, and follow up on it this week.$t$, $t$Lord, help me love my church family like real family.$t$),
(76, $t$From house to house$t$, $t$The early church met in big gatherings and in homes. They worshiped together, and they ate together. The very next verse says the Lord added to their number daily.

That's not an accident. A church where people share life in homes draws others in. Outsiders saw joy and real community, and they wanted it.

The Sunday gathering matters, but it can't hold all of church life. Some of the deepest growth happens around kitchen tables, in living rooms, and over shared meals during the week.

You can be part of this. Invite a church friend and a friend who doesn't know Jesus to the same dinner. Let them see what gospel community looks like.$t$, $t$Plan a meal that brings together someone from church and someone you're reaching.$t$, $t$Lord, fill our homes with gladness, and add to Your church.$t$),
(77, $t$As we have opportunity$t$, $t$Paul ends his letter with a simple charge: when you have a chance to do good, take it. Do good to everyone, and especially to your church family.

Opportunities are everywhere, and most are small. A meal for a family with a new baby. A ride for an older neighbor. A text to someone who's been on your mind. A card on someone's birthday.

These small acts of good add up to a life that points to Jesus. They open doors for conversations and build trust. They remind people they're seen and loved.

You won't take every opportunity, and that's okay. Just take the next one. Then the next.$t$, $t$Look at what's coming up for your people this week: birthdays, needs, and next steps. Choose one way to do good.$t$, $t$Lord, show me today's opportunity, and help me take it.$t$);